# Experiment B-3: AOSS power-collapse knobs (m2468, 2026-11-05)
#
# Lead: mainline's drivers/soc/qcom/qcom_aoss.c exposes debugfs knobs that tell
# the AOP/AOSS firmware to stop power collapsing a rail:
#
#   drivers/soc/qcom/qcom_aoss.c
#     { "prevent_aoss_sleep",    "{class: aoss_slp, res: sleep: %s}", true, "enable", "disable" },
#     { "prevent_cx_collapse",   "{class: cx_mol,   res: cx,   val: %s}", true, "mol", "off" },
#     { "prevent_ddr_collapse",  "{class: ddr_mol,  res: ddr,  val: %s}", true, "mol", "off" },
#
#   Nodes appear at /sys/kernel/debug/qcom_aoss/{prevent_*}.
#   They are bool (kstrtobool_from_user), so the input is 1/0 -- "mol"/"off" are
#   the strings the driver sends to AOSS internally. Reading the node returns
#   EINVAL (write-only).
#
# Hypothesis being tested: the modem PLL assertion
#   (mcpm_resrc_modem_pll.c:1213 clk_mpss_req_status == CLOCK_SUCCESS,
#    "put offline pll back to run state failed (MSS_OFFLINE_CLK_CNTRL 0xc)")
#   happens because the CX rail power-collapses while the modem is up. If CX is
#   held on, the PLL should not have to re-lock.
#
# Observed (clean boot, crash count 0, then prevent_cx_collapse=1):
#   CX collapse 已禁止
#   基线崩溃=0
#   qmicli --dms-set-operating-mode=low-power   -> success
#   qmicli --dms-set-operating-mode=online      -> success
#   t=7s  Registration state: 'not-registered-searching'  崩溃=1
#   ... (no registration for the next 98 s, crash count stays 1)
#
#   Then four more power-cycle attempts with the knob still on:
#   --- 尝试 1 ---  not-registered-searching 崩溃=3
#   --- 尝试 2 ---  not-registered-searching 崩溃=4
#   --- 尝试 3 ---  not-registered-searching 崩溃=5
#   --- 尝试 4 ---  not-registered-searching 崩溃=6
#
# => With prevent_cx_collapse=1 the DMS power cycle crashed the modem every
#    single time and the radio never came up at all, whereas without the knob the
#    cycle does occasionally succeed (RF then lives 30-40 s).
#
# Verdict: holding CX on does NOT stop the PLL assertion; it makes the bring-up
# strictly worse. The knob was turned back to 0 and the modem restarted
# (state=running, crash count frozen at 6, RF down = stable).
#
# Also verified today: /dev/mem reads of the modem DSM region are blocked
#   ("dd: /dev/mem: Bad address"), so the modem's own QuRT log is not readable
#   from userspace on this kernel configuration.
